# Git history reconstruction

Use this rule when a large working-tree diff needs to become an understandable sequence of commits.

## Before staging

- Inspect the complete working tree, including tracked, untracked, deleted, generated, and binary files.
- Review the full diff and group changes by responsibility: platform/infrastructure, shared components, routes/features, styling, content, fixtures, and generated artifacts.
- Write a proposed commit plan before staging anything. Explain dependencies and why each group belongs together.
- Preserve the working tree as the source of truth. Do not reset, discard, redesign, or rewrite changes merely to make grouping easier.

## Commit boundaries

- Prefer cohesive development steps over one giant site-wide commit, one commit per file, or arbitrary folder-based commits.
- Use partial staging when a file mixes implementation with editorial copy or unrelated changes.
- Make each commit leave the repository in a reasonable state where practical; stage prerequisite schemas, loaders, components, and tests before their consumers.
- Use concise conventional-style messages that name the actual feature or refactor.
- Review every staged diff and run the relevant lint, type-check, test, content-check, or build command before committing when practical.

## Editorial and generated-content boundary

Deployment does not imply editorial approval. Do not commit real content merely because it is present in the working tree or already deployed.

- Commit real Markdown records, personal copy, media, portraits, diagrams, and other public artifacts only when the user has explicitly confirmed they are approved for canonical Git history.
- Treat AI-generated, migrated, refreshed, or generated content as unapproved unless explicitly reviewed and approved.
- Engineering code, schemas, layouts, components, routes, styles, tooling, tests, fixture/demo content, and repository rules may be committed normally.
- When implementation and content share a file, preserve the valid implementation with patch/partial staging and leave uncertain content uncommitted.
- Leave generated caches, build output, and refresh reports uncommitted unless the repository explicitly treats them as canonical fixtures.

## Completion report

After the planned commits:

- Show the resulting commit sequence and summarize each logical step.
- Show what remains uncommitted and explain why each category was excluded.
- Confirm that no unreviewed real content was committed.
- Report validation results and any warnings or limitations.
- Do not push, amend, reset, or change remotes unless the user explicitly asks.
